              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Controls</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remote, KS Fit app, FootSense foot-position mode</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WalkingPad and one review</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Warranty</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WalkingPad, one review and the tracker</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price seen</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$499 ($699 reference)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">walkingpad.com, 5 November 2026</td></tr>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Disputed, or WalkingPad only</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Figure</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4 pt-6">Status</th></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Motor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.75 HP brushless (WalkingPad page); 1.25 HP (Best Buy); 2 HP (Amazon, per the tracker)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Conflict</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Maximum user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">230 lb or 300 lb depending on version</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Two versions; one review advises the lower figure</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt width</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">16.5 in. on the product page; 16 in. in a KingSmith parameter table, per the tracker</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Minor conflict</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Noise</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Under 65 dB (WalkingPad marketing); 40 dB (tracker)</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Unverified</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Supply</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">110 V</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">WalkingPad only</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The motor conflict is the one to notice. WalkingPad’s current A1 Pro page calls it a 3.75 HP
            brushless motor, Best Buy lists 1.25 HP, and the tracker reports 2 HP on Amazon, which it reads as a
            peak figure. Walking-pad motor ratings are rarely stated on a common basis, and a pad limited
            to 3.75 mph does not need much power at walking pace. We would not choose between pads on any
            of these numbers; our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">horsepower guide</a> explains why peak and continuous figures are not comparable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="two-versions" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The 230 lb and 300 lb Versions</h2>
<p>
            The A1 Pro has been sold with two weight ratings. The original was rated at about 220 to
            230 lb; WalkingPad now sells a version rated at 300 lb, and one of its product pages offers
            both. On 5 November 2026 the store’s main A1 Pro listing had a single option, "300 lbs", and
            its stock code ended in "A1PLUS". WalkingPad’s page also mentions an "A1 Plus" in an image
            label without describing it as a separate product. We read that as the 300 lb build sold under
            the A1 Pro name, but WalkingPad does not say so, and we could not find a published list of what
            else differs between the two.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two points follow. If you weigh more than about 220 lb, make sure the listing, the box and the
            label on the machine all say 300 lb before you pay. And treat the weight limit as a ceiling,
            not a target: Treadmill Review Guru, after more than a year with an A1 Pro, suggested the lower
            figure is the more realistic one for long-term durability and cautioned users above about
            250 lb. Heavier walkers have better-suited options in our <a href="/best-walking-pads-for-heavy-people/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads for heavy people</a> roundup, several of them rated to 350 or 400 lb.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="living-with-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Folding, Speed Control and Daily Use</h2>
<p>
            The A1 Pro’s defining feature is the fold. The deck hinges in the middle and folds to roughly
            half its length, so it fits in a cupboard or under a bed where an unfolded pad would not. Two
            front wheels let you tip it and roll it. At about 63 lb it is light enough for most adults to
            move, but heavier than most single-piece pads; if you will move it every day, the Z1’s roughly
            51 lb is worth weighing up.
          </p>
          <p>
            The trade-off of a hinge is that it sits under your feet. The long-term review we read says the
            fold can be felt while walking. That is not a fault, but it is a reason to stand on one before
            you buy if you can.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Speed control</h3>
          <p>
            There are three ways to set the speed. The remote and the free KS Fit app work as you would
            expect. FootSense, WalkingPad’s automatic mode, speeds the belt up when you walk towards the
            front and slows it when you drift back. The same review found it hard to hold a steady pace in
            automatic mode on a deck this size, which matches what owners of other foot-sensing pads
            report; most people who walk at a desk use manual mode. That review also reports that the top
            speed is only available after pairing with the app. Keep the remote somewhere safe: if it is
            lost, you are relying on the app until you buy a replacement.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">At a desk</h3>
          <p>
            The A1 Pro has no handrail or console, so it works best under a standing desk, where the desk
            gives you something to steady yourself on. Set the desk height for walking rather than
            standing; our <a href="/standing-desk-height-for-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">desk height guide</a> explains the difference. A 16.5-inch belt is fine for walking in a straight line but narrow if
            you turn to a second monitor, which the long-term review also notes. Walking pads are built for
            this kind of steady, moderate use, not for running, as our guide on <a href="/can-you-run-on-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">running on a walking pad</a> explains.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reviews-say" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What Independent Reviews Report</h2>
<p>
            Search results for "WalkingPad A1 Pro foldable <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>under desk treadmill</a> reviews" are mostly
            retailer pages and pooled Amazon ratings. We list a point here only when it comes from an
            independent review or recurs across sources.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Compact and easy to store.</strong> Every source we read singles out the fold and the 5-inch height.</li>
            <li><strong>Sturdy and quiet for its size.</strong> The long-term review describes it as both; neither WalkingPad’s "under 65 dB" nor the tracker’s 40 dB is a measurement we can check.</li>
            <li><strong>Narrow for some users.</strong> At 16.5 inches it is wider than many pads but narrower than a 20-inch treadmill belt.</li>
            <li><strong>Walking, not long-distance or running use.</strong> The review describes it as unsuited to jogging, running or very long daily sessions.</li>
            <li><strong>Automatic mode takes practice.</strong> Steady pace is easier by remote or app.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Be careful with star ratings. The tracker found that Amazon pools reviews for the A1 Pro in both
            weight versions with the P1 and Z1, so a rating next to the A1 Pro name can describe a different
            machine. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-read-treadmill-reviews/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">reading treadmill reviews</a> covers pooled listings and other traps.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-newer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>A1 Pro vs Z1, C2 and R3</h2>
<p>
            Prices are those on WalkingPad’s US store on 5 November 2026. Z1 and C2 figures were checked
            for our October 2026 pages.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[480px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Published figure</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">A1 Pro</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Z1</th><th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">C2</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking area</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">47 x 16.5 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">47.6 x 15.7 in.</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">47.2 x 15.7 in.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Top speed</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3.75 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">4 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 3.7 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Max user weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">230 or 300 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">242 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">220 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Weight</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 63 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 51 lb</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">About 55 lb</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Folds in half</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Price at WalkingPad</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$499</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$299</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">$399</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            <strong>Z1.</strong> The same idea, newer and $200 cheaper, with a slightly higher top speed and
            a lighter frame. It gives up width and weight rating. For a walker under about 240 lb it is the
            better value; our <a href="/walkingpad-z1-review/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">WalkingPad Z1 review</a> sets out its figures and the motor and noise claims that do not hold up.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>C2.</strong> Close to the Z1 on figures, $100 more, with colour choices and desk
            bundles. Buy it for those reasons rather than for the hardware.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>R3.</strong> Amazon describes the R3 family as a newer model than the A1 Pro, but it is a
            different kind of machine: a wider, faster pad that does not fold in half. WalkingPad listed the
            R3 at $649 for a 265 lb version and $699 for a 330 lb version on 5 November. If you want a little
            jogging and have room to store a full-length deck, it is worth a look; if folding is the point,
            it is not a replacement.
          </p>
          <p>
            Pads from other brands, including incline models and ones with a handrail, are ranked in our <a href="/best-walking-pads/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best walking pads</a> roundup, and our <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs treadmill</a> guide covers when a full treadmill is the better buy.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-used" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Buying a WalkingPad A1 Pro Secondhand</h2>
<p>
            Because the A1 Pro has been on sale for six years, a lot of them are on resale sites. A used
            one can be good value, but a walking pad’s warranty rarely transfers, and the parts that wear
            are the belt, the deck and the motor. Check these before you hand over money:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The weight rating on the label.</strong> The label under or behind the deck should say whether it is the 230 lb or the 300 lb version. Sellers often do not know.</li>
            <li><strong>Run it at full speed for several minutes.</strong> Listen for grinding or a rising whine, and watch for the belt hesitating when you step on. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slows-down-when-i-step-on-it/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt that slows when you step on it</a> explains what that means.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the belt for fraying and drift.</strong> A belt that wanders to one side needs adjusting and may have been neglected.</li>
            <li><strong>Fold and unfold it.</strong> The hinge should lock firmly with no play.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask for the remote and the safety items.</strong> A missing remote means relying on the app.</li>
            <li><strong>Ask about lubrication.</strong> Walking pads need occasional silicone under the belt; our <a href="/how-to-lubricate-a-walking-pad/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad lubrication guide</a> covers how and how often.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            On price, a used A1 Pro only makes sense well below WalkingPad’s $499 new price, because a new
            Z1 is $299 with a warranty. Our <a href="/used-treadmill-buying-checklist/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">used treadmill checklist</a> covers the general checks, and <a href="/how-long-do-walking-pads-last/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how long walking pads last</a> helps you judge how much life an older one has left.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy the WalkingPad A1 Pro</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Buy it if folding storage is essential and you weigh more than the Z1 allows.</strong> The 300 lb version’s rating and the 16.5-inch belt are the real reasons to pay $200 more than
            the Z1.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy the Z1 instead</strong> if you are under about 240 lb and want the same fold for
            less, or if you will move the pad every day and want it lighter.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Look beyond WalkingPad</strong> if you want incline, a handrail, a top speed above
            4 mph or a weight rating above 300 lb; our roundups of <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with incline</a> and <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pads with a handrail</a> cover those.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Buy it at $499 or less.</strong> WalkingPad’s $699 reference price is not what it sells
            for, and our <a href="/black-friday-walking-pad-deals/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad Black Friday guide</a> explains how to judge a November discount.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Common Mistakes Buying a WalkingPad A1 Pro</h2>
          <p>The A1 Pro is simple to buy, but these errors catch people out because of its age and its two versions.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Not checking which weight version you are buying</h3>
              <p>The 230 lb and 300 lb versions share a name. If you are over about 220 lb, confirm 300 lb on the listing, the box and the machine label before paying.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting a pooled star rating</h3>
              <p>Amazon pools reviews for several WalkingPad models on one listing, so a rating shown next to the A1 Pro may be about the Z1 or P1. Read the reviews themselves, or an independent review.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying close to the reference price</h3>
              <p>WalkingPad shows $699 as the reference and sold it at $499 on 5 November 2026. A third-party listing near $699 is not a bargain, and a used one should cost well under $499.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a fold-in-half pad when you do not need the fold</h3>
              <p>The hinge is the reason to buy an A1 Pro and the part you feel underfoot. If you have room to store a flat pad, a single-piece deck may be cheaper and feel more solid.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The bottom line</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">The WalkingPad A1 Pro is still on sale from WalkingPad and still a sound
            fold-in-half walking pad</strong>, with a 47 by 16.5-inch deck, a 300 lb version and a folded length
            of about 32.5 inches. It walks only, it has no incline, and its motor rating is disputed.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buy it at $499 or less, confirm the 300 lb version if you need it, and compare it with the <a href="/walkingpad-z1-review/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">WalkingPad Z1</a>,
            which folds the same way for $200 less and suits most walkers under about 240 lb.
          </p>
        </div>

