        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the WalkingPad A1 Pro discontinued?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Not by WalkingPad. On 5 November 2026 WalkingPad’s US store listed the A1 Pro as in stock at $499 against a $699 reference price. Best Buy no longer sells it new and some Amazon listings have gone, so availability elsewhere is patchy.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit of the WalkingPad A1 Pro?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It depends on the version. The A1 Pro is sold in a 230 lb and a 300 lb version, and on 5 November 2026 WalkingPad’s main listing offered only the 300 lb one. Check the listing and the label on the machine. One long-term review suggests the lower figure is the more realistic one for durability.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How fast does the WalkingPad A1 Pro go?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              WalkingPad lists 0.5 to 3.75 mph (6 km/h); some listings round it to 3.7 mph. It is a walking machine with no incline. One review reports that the top speed is only available after pairing the pad with the KS Fit app.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What motor does the WalkingPad A1 Pro have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Sources disagree. WalkingPad’s current page calls it a 3.75 HP brushless motor, Best Buy lists 1.25 HP, and an independent tracker reports 2 HP on Amazon. Because the figures conflict we do not quote one, and at walking speeds the rating matters less than the weight limit and warranty.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>WalkingPad A1 Pro vs Z1: which should I buy?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Both fold in half. The A1 Pro has a slightly wider 16.5-inch walking area and a 300 lb version; the Z1 is about 12 lb lighter, reaches 4 mph and is rated at 242 lb. On 5 November 2026 WalkingPad sold the A1 Pro at $499 and the Z1 at $299, so the Z1 is the better value for walkers under about 240 lb.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the WalkingPad A1 Pro need assembly?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. WalkingPad lists it as fully assembled. Unfold it, plug it into a 110 V outlet, and pair the remote or the KS Fit app.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

